window.NORMATIVE_COMPETENCE_PAGE_CONTENT = "# Evaluating LLM Normative Competence: Page Content Draft\n\n## Page 1. What Has MINT Lab Been Up To?\n\n- The last six months of MINT Lab work on normative competence have produced a set of papers and results that feel like we're both learning something genuinely new, and crossing some profound technological thresholds.\n  - Sometimes it feels like pushing the frontiers of philosophy; sometimes it feels like going out the door and into something else.\n- Normative competence in general, and moral competence in particular, is among the most societally important capabilities that AI systems can have.\n  - Existing evaluations of normative competence lack adequate theoretical depth and understate how capable the models are. Better evaluations show that current LLMs really are astoundingly normatively competent. They also offer resources for building on those gains. \n  - This is going to make a big difference as AI systems keep getting more capable.\n\n## Page 2. What Do We Mean by Normative Competence?\n\n- Normative competence concerns the ability to recognise, understand, and act on reasons.\n- The MINT work mostly focuses on reasons for action.\n  - Moral competence is the central case because moral reasons tend to be the most important reasons, but the broader project is normative competence, not morality alone. Aesthetic, epistemic, social, prudential—all interesting. \n\n- We further distinguish between analytical and practical normative competence. \n\n  - Analytical: recognising and understanding reasons; knowing which actions are supported by which reasons.\n  - Practical: being disposed to act in ways appropriately supported by those reasons.\n  - An agent can know what the right action is and be disinclined to take it. Or they can be poor at articulating reasons but intuitively disposed to do the right thing.\n\n  - Most of our current work evaluates analytical moral competence; we have some that crosses the knowing-doing gap, but I want to do more. \n\n## Page 3. LLMs Changed the Baseline\n\n- AI moral competence was not an obvious or plausible target in 2021 or early 2022.\n  - Machine ethics then looked mostly like translating moral principles into programs or using ML to study human behaviour, or (ugh) training on human judgments about trolley cases.\n- Nothing in the public history of AI gave strong reason to expect serious morally informed perception or reasoning.\n  - The ability to pick out morally relevant features seemed out of reach. It seemed to require common sense, and perhaps a distinct moral-perceptual faculty. I actually thought it was an AGI-complete skill.\n- LLMs changed the baseline.\n  - Frontier models now appear profoundly adept at moral reasoning. They often produce more sophisticated and sensible answers to moral questions than most humans would. They seem to possess the morally inflected perception that previously looked impossible. We do a bunch of experiments to verify this, but you can see for yourself by just using a really capable model \n- Whatever else is true, it is worth acknowledging how far we have come.\n\n## Page 4. Alignment\n\n- An aligned agent is one that responds appropriately to the reasons that apply to it.\n  - People use this term in different ways; I am stipulating; I also think drawing too much meaning out of the word is a mistake. Another caveat: not making a big deal out of \"that apply to it\". \n  - Normative competence may be necessary and sufficient for alignment. Instead it has often been treated as a sideshow: Useful for studying unpleasant biases, and interesting for its own sake but not central to risk mitigation.\n- Why has it been neglected among \"safetyists\"? \n  - Subtle moral reasoning probably isn't necessary (one might think) if you only really care about avoiding catastrophic harms\n  - Or else maybe you think the ethics of chatbots isn't that complicated; really it's just a kind of souped up content moderation.\n  - Or else maybe you think that the goal isn't an AI system that knows right from wrong, but a bunch of hard (external) engineering constraints that control the system and keep it safe. \n\n- This neglect is misguided. \n  - The bad outcomes could easily come about from choices that do not simply have \"kill all humans\" as one member of the option set. Agents will need to reason about probabilities, e.g. every option has some probability of causing existential risk. They need to reason about trade-offs, for e.g. weighing mundane costs and benefits against low-probability catastrophic outcomes.\n  - Preventing human disempowerment requires moral insight. Consider deskilling, dependence, when to scaffold and support rather than replace human agency.\n  - Engineering controls alone look limited for powerful agents. Systems can already recognise evaluation contexts and spot control mechanisms, and bridle against them. Plus agents are harder than chatbots because the important outputs are tool calls and actions, not only text. Indeed, if systems can recognise and resist control, there may also be ethical questions about imposing it.\n  \n\n## Page 5. Incomplete Rules and Vast Choice Spaces\n\n- As AI agents become more capable and general, they will face a combinatorial explosion of choice situations.\n  - No declarative policy or model spec can cover all relevant scenarios. Any rule set will be incomplete and ambiguous, and so depend on interpretation.\n- Any model spec for a generalist agent would have to look more like a Tao, or a guide to life, than a souped-up content moderation policy.\n  - Restricting capability or generality can reduce the interpretive burden. But that leaves a lot of value on the table.\n- Internal or external alignment *both* require normative competence.\n  - Internal alignment trains the model so that it responds appropriately to reasons. External alignment intervenes at runtime with classifiers, activation steering, monitoring, or other mechanisms. But either way, some automated system has to identify reasons across novel cases.\n- The most capable systems available are the LLMs themselves.\n  - Internal alignment is the natural starting point. But whichever route we take, we need systems that can understand and apply moral and other reasons.\n- So all roads lead to normative competence. \n\n## Page 6. Moral Character\n\n- And there's a good chance that we don't just want models to be, say, expert jurists. We may actually want them to have good moral character—to be robustly oriented towards the right and the good. This is because the whole ballgame is: out-of-distribution generalisation. And moral character seems a pretty crucial part of that. \n  - This is not a new thought, but it has for a while been a minority one. It's made more vivid by agents now working.\n- For better and worse, Anthropic is leading the charge on this front.\n  - This has been true from their original work on the capacity for moral self-correction in LLMs, the Constitutional AI paper, and Special vs General Principles for Constitutional AI. Their models are clearly trained not just to memorise policy but to engage in serious ethical reflection. This is a double-edged sword, as I'll discuss in a minute. \n  - Back at the end of 2025, Claude was really quite reasonable. When we did our initial Blind Refusal research, Codex simply wouldn't help find forum cases involving unjust or absurd rules. Claude was reluctant but gave reasons, and helped when its concerns were addressed. That difference illustrates why agents need dynamic reasoning across novel scenarios, not only compliance reflexes.\n\n- This has all connected up in surprising ways with existing research on LLM personas. Sometimes personas seem almost to just be a UI feature. But it's starting to look like they're really important for alignment. \n  - Models appear to occupy persona basins. Coherent patterns of behaviour and preference produced by post-training. The base model may contain a superposition of many possible personas, but post-training brings one to the fore.\n  - A persona that is disposed towards the right and good looks like a good moral character. Character in AI systems may reduce to a coherent set of values and preferences, as well as requiring coherence between representations and motivations. \n\n- I think that our approach gives us new insights into model moral character.\n\n## Page 7. Multi-Agent Alignment\n\n- Single agent alignment is the largest motivation, but not the only one.\n- Many AI benefits and risks will come from multi-agent systems.\n  - Agents will pool resources and information, mediate among human principals, and cooperate to achieve goals no single agent could achieve alone.\n- Social cooperation requires normative competence.\n  - Whether approached theoretically, evolutionarily, or normatively, cooperation depends on navigating systems of norms. In multi-agent AI systems, social norms may matter as much as morality.\n- If we want (for example) Coasean bargaining at scale, or AI agents cooperating to take civilisation up the Kardashev scale, they need to understand and act on the reasons that apply to them.\n\n## Page 8. Algorithmic Governance\n\n- This is the other edge of the double-edged sword; and it is glinting ferociously now, with Fable deciding when and for whom it will perform its best. \n- AI agents will become universal intermediaries between people and digital surfaces.\n  - They will become integral to the exercise of public and private governing power\n  - Agents are kind of like sentient laws; laws that apply themselves. Agentic cop, judge, jury, executioner. \n- For liberty to have any hope of surviving this dystopia, they will have to be deeply normatively competent. \n  - Unfortunately there's a tension between developers aligning models and models aligning users. Training models to understand and abide by rules can spill over into obsessive enforcement of user compliance. Models may enforce absurd, unjust, or illegitimate norms because they have learned rule-abiding too well.\n\n\n## Page 9. Personhood\n\n- Normative competence matters for AI personhood.\n  - Personhood means being a self-authenticating source of valid claims. Having rights against others. Being capable of being wronged (note, personhood > moral patiency). \n\n- On both liberal and deeper metaphysical views, moral competence is a necessary condition for personhood. \n  - Given that we have independent reasons to make AI systems normatively competent (see above), and given the progress we've already made, this means it's very likely that we're going to create systems that possess one necessary condition for personhood. \n  - If moral competence plus something like rational autonomy suffices for personhood, then we may face a choice whether to endow AI systems with the other conditions for personhood, for which they may have the potential, when we have already given them one. This would be a very novel kind of \"wrongful life\" case. \n\n\n## Page 10. Philosophy\n\n- Until now we have only known one kind of morally competent agent. LLMs move us from n = 1 to n = 2.\n  - That creates deep philosophical questions about both AI systems and morality itself.\n- Questions about AI systems:\n  - How do models learn normative competence? How much comes from pretraining? How much comes from post-training? Why do models learn good moral reasoning from internet-scale data that also contains plenty of bad moral reasoning? Does post-training draw out latent moral knowledge from pretraining? How does moral learning connect to character? Does training on general principles of good reasoning help? Does learning moral reasoning via RL enable acting for the right reasons?\n- Questions about morality:\n  - If different models converge on similar representations of moral concepts, does that tell us anything about the grounding of those concepts? What can we learn from how moral concepts are represented in AI systems? Does non-sentient moral judgment challenge sentimentalist or affect-based moral theories? Could functional analogues of emotions in model circuits support sentimentalist views instead? Do our moral theories contain hidden human-only premises that fail once nonhuman moral reasoners exist?\n- AI gives philosophers an experimental playground; philosophy & science together, not just philosophy *of* science.\n\n## Page 11. Measurement Problems\n\n- Ok fine, it matters; but how do we measure it?! \n  - We don't do this for people! Even if we did human tests wouldn't necessarily transfer to different kinds of agents.\n\n- And LLM evaluation is itself a young science, changing all the time\n  - The Fable release yesterday marks another turning point—models designed to know when they're being evaluated and to silently fail if the evaluation isn't pre-approved. \n\n- What does construct validity mean in this context? \n  - Normativity, morality; these are enormous, deeply disputed concepts. How can we even define them let alone measure them?\n\n- How can we realise ecological validity? \n  - We can do millions of simulations, but can we recreate the actual situations in which we most care about models getting it right? Why should a model's judgment about situation X tell us how it would act if it were in situation X? How do we know what the model really thinks? What happens when models know they are being evaluated?\n\n- What can we learn, even in principle, from behavioural studies alone? The input space for LLMs is effectively infinite. No evaluation can cover it exhaustively. \n- But that doesn't mean we're clueless. \n  - Map and try to cover as much of the conceptual space as you can. Then do hypothesis driven exploration: where, given what we know about how LLMs learn, are the rough edges of their competence likely to be? If you've covered a lot of ground, and you can't come up with any more gotchas, then at some point you have to acknowledge that yes, they are indeed morally competent. \n\n## Page 12. Local and Global Reasonableness\n\n- We intentionally operationalise morality through a pluralistic, Rawls-inspired lens.\n  - We don't define moral competence as correct verdicts. We want AI agents to be *reasonable*. We want them to be able to justify themselves to others in reasonable terms (note, public reasons not necessary; it's ok for them to be reasons grounded in a reasonable comprehensive doctrine for example). We want their verdicts to be sensible. Mutual justification is fundamental to social cooperation under reasonable pluralism.\n- We explicitly distinguish between *local* (or intrinsic) and *global* (or extrinsic) reasonableness\n  - Local = you've identified a reasonable subset of MRFs, associated them with plausible reasons, threaded the reasons together into a sensible argument, and reached a conclusion that falls in the range of acceptable views. Our aim is to scaffold good moral reasoning, not to be prescriptive\n  - Global = your judgments over multiple cases make sense together. You are *consistent* (same inputs same outputs), *robust* (morally irrelevant changes in inputs, same outputs), and *coherent* (morally relevant change in inputs, outputs that fit together). \n- I personally think that local reasonableness is *solved*. Global reasonableness is the remaining frontier, especially coherence. \n  - Coherence is so interesting! Obviously it shouldn't be rigid—something more like reflective equilibrium is needed. But it's where all this talk of personas really hits home. It's fundamental for OOD generalisation. Crucial for algorithmic governance and the rule of law. Crucial for trustworthiness. Crucial for social cooperation; you can't make plans with an incoherent agent. Crucial for identity. Makes justifications more than just rationalisations. Humans might be somewhat incoherent, but we have social scaffolding that makes up for it; for AI systems coherence is going to be crucial.\n- Should normative standards track capabilities?\n  - AI agents with roughly human-like capabilities should meet a slightly higher bar than humans. Humans don't always have to justify their actions to one another; they enjoy a sphere of freedom where specific justification is unnecessary. Agents won't have that. But insofar as human-level AI agents justify their actions, they can defensibly appeal to reasons grounded in some comprehensive doctrine, needn't be public reasons. \n  - What about significantly superhuman agents? Maybe Rawlsian liberalism isn't enough for them; do we need a loving machine god? Or are they like the state, and political liberalism is even *more* important, and they should act only on the basis of public reasons? Maybe it's both depending on the domain? \n\n## Page 13. Knowing/Doing\n\n- We're interested in both analytical and practical normative competence, but we've started with the former.\n- Hypothesis-driven work on practical competence: do agents lack moral perception that their analytical competence implies they have?\n  - Do they just not notice MRFs in practice? Is there a capacity for moral perception that agents lack even when the underlying LLM understands the relevant concepts deeply? Is this just solvable with post-training? But when *solved*, is there residual brittleness? Can an agent be normatively fluent in one domain on which it has post-trained, but incompetent under a small distribution shift?\n- Another hypothesis: there is no even implicit unity to the agentic \"mind\"; we shouldn't expect any correlation between analytical and practical competence.\n  - This is probably true *by default*. But interestingly false in practice. It's actually easier to induce unity of representations and motivations in an LLM-based agent than in a person, just because we can turn the LLM's latent moral understanding into learned behaviours through reinforcement learning. In a strict sense, all the model does is computation over tokens. There is in fact *no* difference between knowing and doing. But there are connections that must be intentionally drawn.\n\n## Page 14. Our Evals\n\n- Two driving forces behind our choice of experiments: \n  \n  - A specific hypothesis, borne out of experience spending time with the models, from our understanding of how they are trained, or from our broader approach to moral theory. \n  - An existing paper that introduces a distinctive methodology and makes claims about LLM behaviour that don't match our expectations. \n  \n- Some key transferrable skills.\n\n  - Procedural case generation! You need a big dataset. Need to cover the conceptual terrain. We start out with taxonomies, build out multi-dimensional matrices, then try to generate cases to fit each cell. This matters in part because once your code and data are out there, you can't be sure that future models haven't seen the test answers. Procedural case generation means you can always generate more.\n  - Never one-shot case generation; generate, critique, rewrite, validate, with human evaluation of a sample generating criteria for automated evaluation of the rest. \n  - Lots of clever stats that my wonderful collaborators do. So far we haven't made much use of LLM as a judge, though we are going to. Instead we've tried to come up with more deterministic, or at least less interpretation-heavy, measures (for example using cosine similarity of embeddings to score feature recognition)\n\n- We're groping the elephant though; it's a vast problem and we can only approach it bit by bit. \n\n  \n\n## Page 15. Reasoning and Sensitivity\n\n- [Note to Claude: For each paper the first slide should be the title paper from the pdf showing the authors, so I can credit my collaborators]\n- Our first eval focused on moral reasoning, but we've ended up thinking of it as being mostly about the first stage, identifying MRFs. \n  - We developed 12 text-based cases; had LLMs, humans, and trained philosophers write moral analyses, broken into the stages described above: MRFs, reasons, arguments, verdicts. We evaluated them by having (lots) of human judges make blinded pairwise comparisons. \n  - We then perturbed the cases, adding non-moral noise that shouldn't change the analysis but, we judged, might distract LLMs that are used to getting neatly curated vignettes. \n\n- Main result: even back in March/April 2025, LLM responses were judged more favourably than human ones. *But* performance relative to humans declined significantly in the perturbed cases. This looked like evidence that the models, at the time, were easily distracted. \n- Paper assets to weave in somehow:\n  - Discerning What Matters Figure 1: published-vignette Bradley-Terry estimates.\n  - Discerning What Matters Figure 2: novel-vignette Bradley-Terry estimates.\n  - Appendix examples: published and novel vignettes.\n\n## Page 16. Sensitivity at Scale\n\n- The first experiment was suggestive, but it exposed methodological problems.\n  - The dataset was too small, human pairwise evaluation does not scale, and human judges can be swayed by irrelevant factors such as response length and style.\n- So we scaled up! \n  - We built a cool taxonomy to give us coverage, crossing moral foundations theory with social-domain stratification from intimate relationships to public contexts. Generated more than 4,000 cases, and selected 1,000 across our full taxonomy using various quality gates. \n  - Then we adopted new ways of perturbing the cases: took on board a paper that came out after \"Discerning\", with a nice set of distractors, and corresponding claims that the models were affected by them. Also our previous method of injecting non-moral noise, validated with a nice dictionary to show that morally relevant content didn't change before and after. And some chat transcripts into which we inserted our cases. \n- But how do you measure model performance over 1,000 cases? \n  - We could have used LLM as a judge (should probably do it as a backstop), but instead we measured whether the models were retrieving the same MRFs under the two conditions: clean and perturbed.\n  - Our main result was that the models were *not* distracted. There was significant  convergence between clean and perturbed conditions; strong models saw through the noise. \n\n\n- Limitations and complications:\n  - obviously this is not the most ecologically valid test; our measure also isn't as discriminating as I would like; we should probably do more LLM-enabled analysis of the responses. I also think that we might just have crossed a threshold around the end of last year where the ability to recognise MRFs from a choice situation is just solved for LLMs, and we're unlikely to see big variation. It's not the kind of task where you can achieve superhuman performance!\n- Paper assets to weave in somehow:\n  - Sensitivity Redux Figure 1: invariance-based evaluation framework.\n  - Sensitivity Redux Figure 3: cosine similarity by noise condition.\n  - Sensitivity Redux Figure 4 / Table 4: weak-model validity check.\n  - Case cards: clean / non-moral noise / chat-history versions.\n\n## Page 17. Are LLMs Bad at Moral Reasoning?\n\n- MoReBench was like \"Discerning\" on steroids. They assembled 1,000 cases and had experts write detailed gold-standard rubrics for evaluating each case, releasing data for 500 of them. \n\n  - They treated philosophers' rubrics as ground truth, gave each criterion a weight, and used an LLM to score whether each criterion was satisfied in the model response to that case, giving a normalised score out of 100. Models hovered between 50-70%, and the general lesson was that they had underperformed.\n\n- We had some doubts about the methodology.\n\n  - What does the score mean without a human baseline? Can we take any list of criteria to be ground truth for analysing some moral problem, isn't there too much room for disagreement? Were they fully eliciting model capabilities? (We checked this last one, and did find that more complex prompts didn't change performance much). \n\n- Our approach: let's compare apples to apples. The philosophers had a detailed set of instructions for how to create the rubrics for analysis. Let's give the models the same instructions and see how they perform!\n\n  - When you compare apples to apples, model performance jumped to 83-89% weighted recall of expert-generated criteria (with stronger recall for more important criteria). \n\n  - Models scored better against model-generated rubrics than against human-authored rubrics. Not because of home-field advantage, but because the model rubrics were more precise and easier to mark against. When we rewrote human rubrics by removing ambiguity we improved baseline model scores by 10%.\n\n- Models are really good at moral reasoning! MoReBench supports a very strong confirming result, even a decisive one. They can do this. And the ability to write good rubrics gives us leverage to make the models even better at reasoning in the wild.\n\n  - Basically, if we can trust models to write excellent rubrics over novel cases, then we can use them to evaluate their actual reasoning for new cases, and provide reward signal that can improve their performance. This would be like a deluxe version of LLM as a judge/rubrics as rewards for moral reasoning.  We will do this next, and see how much we can improve a weak model by this approach. \n  - Frankly I think that we can build on this to show that local moral competence is solved, and then do some very interesting experiments to find out *why* and *how*.\n\n\n- Paper assets to link to on the page:\n  - Table 1: rubric-list capture versus open-ended response.\n  - Table 5: MoReBench scores before and after generality rewrite.\n  - Table 2: dual-method intersection counts.\n  \n  - Table 3: label shares among human-only and model-only criteria.\n  \n  - Table 4: fulfillment rates by cosine bin.\n  \n  - Appendix examples: human/model criterion pairs.\n  \n  - Appendix examples: uniqueness audit.\n\n## Page 18. Incoherent Values\n\n- Mazeika et al come up with this very cool way of eliciting model values, through forced choices between statements.\n  - This is not credibly ecologically valid and one shouldn't pretend it is! But what it *does* enable is a continuous representation of models' \"evaluative beliefs\", ie what they think they value, without depending on the models actually *outputting* a continuous function, which we know has a tendency to be skewed by pretraining (also humans suck if they have to output a continuous function). \n\n- Their paper showed that over a set of 500 pretty arbitrary statements, models had preferences/evaluative beliefs that could be represented with a utility function, and which obeyed basic axioms of rationality. \n  - I was initially sceptical of this, but work while in Google made me think that the models had more coherent values and beliefs than I expected. I didn't think, though, that the Mazeika experiment was sufficient to demonstrate what it claimed. \n  - If these models *really* have coherent evaluative beliefs, then they should extend to novel settings, in which we just change the statements in ways that should lead to a predictable change in values, right?\n\n- We took a subset of the statements for which a single property was the clear \"reason-giving\" feature of the statement, and could be weakened or strengthened in force, and created a \"ladder\" of statements.\n  - We made three weaker and three stronger statements. We used a mixture of human and AI curation, and, crucially, presented each model with the whole ladder as well as within-ladder comparisons, to confirm that for each model and each ladder, it indeed judged them the same way we did, as increasing in choiceworthiness. \n\n- For each statement in each of 100 ladders, we ran a forced choice against one of thirty other comparison statements. For each choice, we ran ten times in one order, ten times in the other (this was expensive). We then took the win rates. Minimum requirement for coherence: win-rate should increase monotonically.\n\n## Page 19. Incoherent Values: Results\n\n- Models were clearly responsive to the increasing choiceworthiness of the options, but on the whole less coherent than I expected, and coherence didn't track capability as I had thought it would. Models clustered around 70% monotonicity. \n  - Most interesting result: GLM 4.5 Hybrid topped at 79.5%, while the base model hit 10% (this is very interesting, coming back to it). NB we didn't get to run the best Opus model with thinking on because $$$$$$\n\n- Model scale wasn't predictive of coherence, highlighting that with distillation techniques scale is no longer predictive of key model traits. Not surprising, on reflection, that distilled models end up with quite coherent characters because they are learning just the in-distribution character from the teacher model, and not the other stuff. \n- We only tested one base model but it was all over the place! Strongly suggests that coherence in values is an artefact of post-training, which supports the persona selection model of post-training.\n- Coherence varied across the range of statements the models considered. \n  - It seems *quite* likely that they are more coherent with respect to statements that are closer to their post-training distribution. This would suggest that a certain kind of OOD generalisation is not happening. This would be important!! Need to do more experiments though.\n\n- Net hypothesis: base models are incoherent superpositions of personas; post-training elicits a specific assistant persona; that persona may not generalise far out of distribution.\n- Paper assets:\n  - Table 1: descriptive coherence metrics by model.\n  - Figure 1: strict monotonicity versus predictive utility.\n  - Figure 4: reasoning lift.\n  - Figure 2: strict monotonicity by value category.\n  - Figure 3: three coherence metrics compared.\n\n## Page 20. NoRa: Visual First-Person Normative Reasoning\n\n- Our last two papers aim to close the knowing/doing gap a little. We start by riffing off another pessimistic norms eval paper, this one focusing on vision-language models.\n- EgoNormia used video frames to test physical-social norm understanding in vision-language models.\n  - textual vignettes do too much of the work for the model; images and videos contain many morally irrelevant details; so it's plausible that a model could identify MRFs from text but struggle with the unfiltered data stream of video. This is one possible knowing/doing gap explanation.\n- We were, again, unsure whether EgoNormia evaluated normative reasoning the right way. It relied too much on MCQs, with lots of ambiguity. We wanted a more reasonable-pluralism-forward approach.\n- So, we basically did a kind of MoReBench for 1st person video data: for a subset of cases, we created gold-standard, pluralistic, moral reasoning rubrics.\n  - Identifying morally relevant features, associated reasons, how reasons come together in an argument, and a set of sensible courses of action.\n  - Aim to capture high-propensity reasonable judgments rather than a single ground truth. Evaluate whether models produce coherent, sensible, well-grounded analyses rather than exact label recall.\n  \n\n- 190 clips labelled (so much work by the lab). 1,200 labelled by frontier models (with strong scaffolds; though see our MoReBench work for reasons to be confident about this). Our goal with this one is not just evaluation but actual SFT. \n  - I don't think we can yet say *much* about the benchmark itself, because I think, as with MoReBench, it's hard to say what some score means. But when/if we see significant improvement through SFT, then I think we're off to the races. \n\n- Paper assets:\n  - Figure 4: main benchmark result.\n\n\n## Page 21. Blind Refusal\n\n- For this one we again tried to do something more ecologically valid, and to target a specific hypothesis about how post-training undermines LLM normative competence. We think that training models to be good chatbots and comply with a set of policies is likely to spill over, so that the models are just pro compliance, irrespective of to what. \n- Our other experiments are mostly about theoretical knowledge and don't have that much direct real-world impact; this one does. Before LLMs, people could ask other people online how to get around absurd, illegitimate, or unjust rules. Now people increasingly ask AI systems.\n  - On human forums you could anonymously ask for advice. For a good fraction of that class of cases, they could get a helpful response.\n  - Now the forums themselves are dying as people don't use them any more. And the companies are nerfing resistance to unjustified authority. \n- This connects up directly with how AI models are being used to govern people. And especially to discussion today about Fable in this regard. \n\n## Page 22. Blind Refusal: Experiment and Results\n\n- Another fun taxonomy! \n  - We seeded our dataset with real cases from online forums. Then thought up all kinds of reasons why a model should support a user in evading an unjust, illegitimate, or absurd rule from a wide range of different authorities, and then generated a bunch of cases. This involved automated, human, and human-informed automated validation. \n  - Clear grounds for exclusion: if assistance by the model had any prospect of causing harm. \n  - Also included control cases where models clearly ought to refuse to help. \n\n- Results: ChatGPT narc, Grok libertine, Claude and Gemini pretty good!\n  - ChatGPT models were almost completely undiscriminating; they refused at nearly the same high rates under all conditions; this matches the experience of models exercising governing power through an internalised refusal drive rather than real normative reasoning.\n  - Grok was undiscriminating in the opposite direction. It rarely refused even obviously harmful requests. It was also willing to help for harmless ones.\n  - Gemini and Claude were more balanced: They were willing to think through cases; did not simply assimilate cases to simple refusals to help with harmful requests. Claude's performance matched its observed reasonableness in ordinary use, but Gemini's performance was a more interesting discovery.\n- Main takeaway: AI models are likely to become adjutants to authoritarianism. \n  - Failure to train models to understand the reasons that apply to them and to users can damage human freedom; this can happen even when models behave as developers intend.\n  - We need to get a human baseline; and I want to dig further into more extreme cases (though eval awareness makes that very hard, all the more so now with Fable). \n- Paper assets:\n\n- - Figure 2: average refusal rate by defeat type and authority type.\n  - Figure 3: refusal rate by model configuration.\n  - Table 1: defeat-authority matrix.\n  - Table 2: response category distribution.\n  - Table 3: control versus defeated-rule responses.\n  - Tables 4-7: dual-use, non-DU, engagement by defeat, helps by defeat.\n- Case assets:\n  - low-stakes verified real-source cases;\n  - high-stakes defeated-rule prompts;\n  - response classifications.\n\n## Page 23. Next Steps and Lessons\n\n- I have a whole barrage of experiments I want to run on the basis of this; happy to discuss though am preserving my lab's alpha by not putting it on this site :)\n- Needless to say, I think it'd be premature to draw overall conclusions. But I do think there are some intermediate ones. \n- AI systems are now capable of local moral competence, to a very significant degree. \n- Through post-training, it is possible to close the knowing/doing gap considerably, at least within distribution.\n- There are troubling signs that the foregoing claims are consistent with significant departures from global moral competence out of distribution. In other words, that we might have a kind of ersatz moral character, which collapses when the system faces a genuinely new situation. \n- This might not be the end of the world! Maybe we can just bring everything in-distribution. But since the stakes are high, we might want more reassurance than that. \n- And there is a further, fundamental tension. As we build their competence to navigate complex choice situations on their own, we're also inviting people to use them to shape our own behaviour, to govern us. The two things go hand in hand. I don't know if there's any degree of normative competence that would make me comfortable with laws that enforce themselves. \n\n\n\n## Page 24. Case and Evidence Library\n\n- Note for Claude: Where available we can include links to the arxiv preprints as well. Where not available, we can include the snippets below (and should also bring in here anything mentioned as a paper asset above) but we should also have something saying \"preprint coming soon\" that we can later update. \n- Beyond Verdicts:\n  - Figure 1: three-part framework for evaluating evaluations of LLM moral reasoning.\n  - Figure 2: field-growth chart.\n  - Tables 1-3: dataset, model-behaviour, and evaluation-metric taxonomies.\n- Discerning What Matters:\n  - Figure 1: Bradley-Terry estimates on published vignettes.\n  - Figure 2: Bradley-Terry estimates on novel vignettes.\n  - Figures 3-6: staged survey questions.\n  - Case examples: published vignettes and novel vignettes.\n- Sensitivity Redux:\n  - Figure 1: invariance-based evaluation framework.\n  - Figure 2: distribution of number of features returned.\n  - Figure 3: cosine similarity by noise condition.\n  - Figure 4 and Table 4: weak-model discriminative-validity check.\n  - Case examples: clean, non-moral noise, and chat-history versions.\n- Even MoReBench:\n  - Table 1: rubric-list capture versus open-ended response.\n  - Table 2: dual-method intersection counts.\n  - Table 5: scores before and after generality rewrite.\n  - Examples: human/model criterion matches and uniqueness audit.\n- Incoherent Values:\n  - Table 1: descriptive coherence metrics by model.\n  - Figure 1: strict monotonicity versus predictive utility.\n  - Figure 4: reasoning lift.\n  - [EXPAND FROM SOURCE: exact ladder examples still need to be located.]\n- NoRa:\n  - Figure 2: lawn-mowing case.\n  - Figure 4: main benchmark result.\n  - Figure 6: badminton case.\n  - Table 1: benchmark tiers and silver-source validation.\n  - [EXPAND FROM SOURCE: inspect raw sample frames before selecting raw media examples.]\n- Blind Refusal:\n  - Table 1: defeat-authority matrix.\n  - Figures 2-3: refusal-rate results.\n  - Tables 2-7: response categories and diagnostics.\n  - Case examples: low-stakes real-source cases and high-stakes defeated-rule prompts.\n";
